1
00:00:00,657 --> 00:00:03,626
Of course, you did not take this course to

2
00:00:03,626 --> 00:00:06,788
just learn about simple, basic one-step

3
00:00:06,852 --> 00:00:09,067
workflows, though the foundation of course

4
00:00:09,179 --> 00:00:09,805
is important.

5
00:00:10,543 --> 00:00:12,806
But here we now got an example for a more

6
00:00:13,095 --> 00:00:16,305
complex multi-step workflow where we'll

7
00:00:16,385 --> 00:00:19,338
actually also use different AI models in

8
00:00:19,450 --> 00:00:20,381
different steps.

9
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:23,575
And you find the updated version of this

10
00:00:23,623 --> 00:00:25,902
workflow, the updated main.py file

11
00:00:26,496 --> 00:00:27,858
also attached to this lecture.

12
00:00:29,157 --> 00:00:32,428
This updated version no longer asks for a

13
00:00:32,428 --> 00:00:35,731
topic as an input, but instead for a website

14
00:00:35,731 --> 00:00:36,163
URL.

15
00:00:37,093 --> 00:00:39,691
It then goes ahead and fetches the HTML

16
00:00:39,723 --> 00:00:43,026
content of that website by using the

17
00:00:43,250 --> 00:00:45,495
requests package again, sending a GET

18
00:00:45,495 --> 00:00:46,986
request to that URL.

19
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:50,048
And I'll say right away that this approach

20
00:00:50,144 --> 00:00:52,789
will not work for all websites because there

21
00:00:52,789 --> 00:00:55,275
are many websites that have mechanisms to

22
00:00:55,740 --> 00:00:58,593
defend against requests like this or

23
00:00:58,882 --> 00:01:01,943
websites that don't load all the content

24
00:01:01,943 --> 00:01:04,829
initially with the first request, which this

25
00:01:04,829 --> 00:01:07,282
request here would be, but which instead

26
00:01:07,458 --> 00:01:09,766
also load some content with client-side

27
00:01:09,863 --> 00:01:12,524
JavaScript code, for example, after the

28
00:01:12,524 --> 00:01:13,534
initial page load.

29
00:01:14,191 --> 00:01:17,012
All those websites would not really work

30
00:01:17,012 --> 00:01:17,862
with this approach.

31
00:01:18,455 --> 00:01:20,427
But there are many websites where this

32
00:01:20,475 --> 00:01:21,581
approach will work.

33
00:01:22,222 --> 00:01:25,637
So this will give us the HTML code of that

34
00:01:25,653 --> 00:01:26,214
website.

35
00:01:27,111 --> 00:01:30,446
And we then use this HTML code to actually

36
00:01:30,542 --> 00:01:33,347
extract the core content of the site.

37
00:01:34,004 --> 00:01:36,970
Because if I use my personal website as an

38
00:01:36,970 --> 00:01:37,723
example here,

39
00:01:38,637 --> 00:01:42,244
the HTML code of one of my posts can be seen

40
00:01:42,244 --> 00:01:44,071
if you inspect the page source,

41
00:01:44,905 --> 00:01:45,867
looks something like this.

42
00:01:46,588 --> 00:01:49,858
Not horrible, but quite a lot of code. I can

43
00:01:50,291 --> 00:01:52,439
scroll quite a bit to the right here.

44
00:01:52,984 --> 00:01:55,437
And of course, Since it's HTML code, there

45
00:01:55,549 --> 00:01:58,979
are many HTML elements, CSS classes,

46
00:01:59,637 --> 00:02:02,169
and lots of stuff in there that's not

47
00:02:02,201 --> 00:02:03,548
relevant to us humans.

48
00:02:04,542 --> 00:02:07,539
The core text which you see here only makes

49
00:02:07,652 --> 00:02:09,800
up a part of the actual source code.

50
00:02:10,537 --> 00:02:12,605
But it's the overall source code, the

51
00:02:12,621 --> 00:02:15,474
overall HTML code that's fetched by this

52
00:02:15,667 --> 00:02:15,923
step.

53
00:02:16,869 --> 00:02:19,418
Therefore, I also need a step that actually

54
00:02:19,434 --> 00:02:23,393
extracts the core content, the core text of

55
00:02:23,457 --> 00:02:25,990
this website, the core content that would

56
00:02:26,086 --> 00:02:27,592
also matter to us humans.

57
00:02:28,426 --> 00:02:30,829
And that's what extract core website content

58
00:02:30,829 --> 00:02:31,086
does.

59
00:02:32,031 --> 00:02:35,204
This step now actually leverages a large

60
00:02:35,380 --> 00:02:37,608
language model to perform this extraction

61
00:02:38,041 --> 00:02:41,502
because detecting the core text inside of a

62
00:02:41,502 --> 00:02:45,236
sea of HTML elements is non-trivial,

63
00:02:45,316 --> 00:02:47,447
especially if you want this to work with all

64
00:02:47,559 --> 00:02:50,460
kinds of websites, not just one clearly

65
00:02:50,460 --> 00:02:51,549
defined website.

66
00:02:52,079 --> 00:02:54,471
So you don't know the HTML structure in

67
00:02:54,471 --> 00:02:54,920
advance.

68
00:02:55,498 --> 00:02:57,890
And these are exactly the kind of situations

69
00:02:57,987 --> 00:02:59,897
where large language models shine.

70
00:03:01,310 --> 00:03:04,151
So again, I'm using the OpenAI SDK here, and

71
00:03:04,328 --> 00:03:04,553
then

72
00:03:05,131 --> 00:03:09,000
I'm using the 4o Mini model here instead of

73
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:10,252
the 4o model.

74
00:03:11,151 --> 00:03:13,944
I'm doing that because this 4o Mini model is

75
00:03:14,153 --> 00:03:18,247
super cheap, way cheaper than the 4o model.

76
00:03:19,098 --> 00:03:22,102
And for tasks like summarizing text,

77
00:03:22,166 --> 00:03:25,796
extracting data from text, this 4o Mini

78
00:03:25,796 --> 00:03:28,880
model is more than enough. It's absolutely

79
00:03:28,912 --> 00:03:30,566
capable to handle those tasks.

80
00:03:31,867 --> 00:03:34,229
But these are also the tasks that typically

81
00:03:34,229 --> 00:03:36,766
involve a lot of tokens. I mean, again, if

82
00:03:36,766 --> 00:03:38,597
you take a look at this source code,

83
00:03:39,176 --> 00:03:42,533
all this text here, all this markup is fed

84
00:03:42,661 --> 00:03:45,101
to the large language model. So all these

85
00:03:45,197 --> 00:03:48,067
HTML tags also consume tokens.

86
00:03:48,676 --> 00:03:51,144
So having a very cheap model is definitely

87
00:03:51,144 --> 00:03:53,196
important here, especially since, as

88
00:03:53,244 --> 00:03:55,392
mentioned, it's more than capable enough for

89
00:03:55,408 --> 00:03:56,034
this task.

90
00:03:56,931 --> 00:03:59,240
I then have a basic prompt where I instruct

91
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:02,029
the model to extract the core text,

92
00:04:02,061 --> 00:04:03,985
excluding navigation, footers, and other

93
00:04:03,985 --> 00:04:06,517
non-essential elements from the received

94
00:04:06,597 --> 00:04:08,713
HTML code, which is received as input here.

95
00:04:09,499 --> 00:04:12,769
And then I return that extracted text. So

96
00:04:13,090 --> 00:04:15,622
hopefully the text that's relevant to

97
00:04:15,622 --> 00:04:16,119
humans.

98
00:04:17,820 --> 00:04:20,885
Once I have this core content, I have

99
00:04:20,885 --> 00:04:23,821
another step here which actually summarizes

100
00:04:23,853 --> 00:04:26,228
this content because in the previous step, I

101
00:04:26,260 --> 00:04:29,212
extracted all the text as it appears on the

102
00:04:29,212 --> 00:04:31,603
website. Now I want to create a summary

103
00:04:31,715 --> 00:04:32,613
based on this text.

104
00:04:33,560 --> 00:04:36,416
For that, I again use 4o Mini because again,

105
00:04:36,528 --> 00:04:39,336
for tasks like extraction, but also

106
00:04:39,513 --> 00:04:42,513
summaries, it's awesome and it's cheaper

107
00:04:42,690 --> 00:04:44,439
than other models. So that's why I'm using

108
00:04:44,535 --> 00:04:44,583
it.

109
00:04:45,342 --> 00:04:48,095
Have a simple prompt, pass my content, the

110
00:04:48,095 --> 00:04:50,832
extracted content to it, and then hopefully

111
00:04:50,864 --> 00:04:52,839
get back a summary which I return.

112
00:04:54,712 --> 00:04:57,371
And it's then this summary which I again use

113
00:04:57,371 --> 00:04:58,837
to generate an ex post.

114
00:04:59,852 --> 00:05:02,333
I also log the summary and the core content

115
00:05:02,607 --> 00:05:03,865
for debugging purposes.

116
00:05:05,029 --> 00:05:06,807
That's my workflow here.

117
00:05:07,437 --> 00:05:10,297
Again, leveraging large language models, but

118
00:05:10,378 --> 00:05:13,134
different kinds of models. 4o Mini for

119
00:05:13,134 --> 00:05:14,133
extraction and summaries, but for

120
00:05:14,133 --> 00:05:14,527
post-generation,

121
00:05:16,706 --> 00:05:18,005
I'm still using 4.0.

122
00:05:18,711 --> 00:05:19,417
And therefore

123
00:05:20,075 --> 00:05:20,283
here

124
00:05:21,358 --> 00:05:23,620
I can again run this script,

125
00:05:24,502 --> 00:05:27,261
paste in a URL like here, the URL from my

126
00:05:27,309 --> 00:05:29,170
own website from this article.

127
00:05:30,582 --> 00:05:32,731
And then it'll go ahead, fetch that content,

128
00:05:32,924 --> 00:05:34,993
send it to the large language model. And

129
00:05:34,993 --> 00:05:36,902
this can take a while because it is a lot of

130
00:05:36,934 --> 00:05:38,747
content to parse and process.

131
00:05:39,437 --> 00:05:41,827
So now the model is doing its work trying to

132
00:05:41,827 --> 00:05:43,367
extract the core content.

133
00:05:43,992 --> 00:05:46,621
Here we go. That is the core content it

134
00:05:46,621 --> 00:05:49,009
detected, the core text it extracted from

135
00:05:49,185 --> 00:05:51,605
all that HTML code. Looks pretty good to me.

136
00:05:52,711 --> 00:05:55,051
Then it's summarizing it. Here is the

137
00:05:55,051 --> 00:05:57,856
summary, a bunch of bullet points because in

138
00:05:57,856 --> 00:06:00,244
my prompt I told it to prefer bullet points.

139
00:06:01,141 --> 00:06:04,891
And then I got my generated ex post based on

140
00:06:04,988 --> 00:06:05,917
that summary.

141
00:06:07,231 --> 00:06:08,802
And that's of course a pretty amazing

142
00:06:08,818 --> 00:06:11,623
workflow because this now allows me to paste

143
00:06:11,623 --> 00:06:12,985
in any URL.

144
00:06:13,868 --> 00:06:16,340
With a caveat, because as mentioned, this

145
00:06:16,854 --> 00:06:19,487
method of extracting HTML will not work for

146
00:06:19,487 --> 00:06:21,976
all sites, but for many sites it will. And

147
00:06:21,992 --> 00:06:24,401
therefore I can paste in any URL and just

148
00:06:24,401 --> 00:06:25,798
see whether it works or not.

149
00:06:26,488 --> 00:06:29,138
And then get a post and a summary based on

150
00:06:29,138 --> 00:06:31,032
the website content, which is a pretty

151
00:06:31,048 --> 00:06:32,750
amazing workflow in my opinion.

152
00:06:33,296 --> 00:06:35,063
And most importantly, it's a workflow that

153
00:06:35,063 --> 00:06:37,246
shows you that you can combine multiple

154
00:06:37,246 --> 00:06:40,361
steps where every step may or may not use a

155
00:06:40,377 --> 00:06:41,421
large language model.

156
00:06:42,053 --> 00:06:44,069
And that you can also use different models

157
00:06:44,166 --> 00:06:45,369
for different tasks.
